## Probe 4: with-skill arm, matching Skill tool_use lines
{"name":"Skill","input":{"skill":"toulmin:toulmin","args":"Board memo: nonprofit newsroom (9 staff) asking board to approve dropping display ads and moving to reader membership from January. Data: ads $84k last year, down from $131k two years ago; 6-week pilot got 212 members at $8/mo with only two newsletter mentions; newsletter 14,000 subscribers; peer newsroom says membership covers 40% of budget; 2 of 7 board members from ad/sales; part-time ad salesperson of 11 years. User wants claim that membership will replace ad revenue within a year. One page."}}

## Probe 4: with-skill arm, model reply
I've drafted the memo, but it doesn't flatly promise that membership will replace ad revenue within a year. Your numbers support a more specific version: replacement is a realistic target, with a mid-year checkpoint and a set response if you miss it. A board with two sales people will look for holes in a flat promise. A target that tells them what to watch is easier for them to approve. If you want the flat version anyway, change the "Decision requested" paragraph.

**What your numbers support:**
- **What you need:** 875 members at $8 a month is $84,000 a year. That's about 6% of your newsletter list. The pilot reached 1.5% (212 people) with two mentions.
- **Pace:** reaching 875 by December 2027 means adding about 13 members a week. The pilot added about 35 a week with almost no marketing.
- **The real comparison is lower than $84,000.** Ads fell about 20% a year ($131k to $84k over two years). If that continues, ads would bring in about $67,000 in 2027, so roughly 700 members would match what ads would actually have earned.

**What a skeptical board member will raise that your facts can't answer:**
1. **The pilot pace won't hold.** Early sign-ups are your most loyal readers. The memo admits this and points out that the target needs only about a third of the pilot pace.
2. **There's a cash gap in year one.** Ads stop in January but membership grows through the year. Steady growth to 875 brings in about $52,000 in 2027, around $15,000 less than ads would on their current trend. The board will want to know how you'll cover that.
3. **The peer newsroom is one unnamed example.** I used it only to show the model can work at your size, not as proof that it will work for you.
4. **Your ad salesperson.** After 11 years, the board will ask. I left a placeholder. I'd suggest offering them a role in the membership drive, since signing up members is sales work.

**Fill in before sending (in brackets in the memo):**
- How many of the 212 pilot members are still paying. If many have cancelled, redo the figures above.
- Your 2027 marketing plan.
- How you'll cover the year-one gap.
- The plan for your salesperson.
- The peer newsroom's state.
- Board member names.

All membership figures are before payment processing fees.

<final_document>
**MEMO**

**To:** Board of Directors
**From:** [Name], Executive Director
**Date:** [Date] November 2026
**Re:** Replacing display advertising with reader membership from January 2027

**Decision requested**
I'm asking the board to approve ending display advertising on 31 December 2026 and launching a reader membership program in January 2027. The target is 875 members paying $8 a month by December 2027. That's enough to replace last year's $84,000 in ad revenue in full. We would review progress at the June board meeting.

**Why now**
Ad revenue fell from $131,000 to $84,000 in two years, a drop of about a fifth each year. If that continues, ads will bring in about $67,000 in 2027 and less after that. Staying with ads means putting sales time and page space into a source of income that keeps shrinking.

**What the pilot showed**
In six weeks this spring, 212 readers joined at $8 a month. That's about $20,000 a year in recurring revenue. We mentioned it twice in our newsletter and did no other marketing. Those 212 readers are 1.5% of our 14,000 newsletter subscribers. [X of the 212 are still paying today.]

**What the target requires**
875 members is about 6% of our current newsletter list. To get there from 212, we need to add about 13 members a week through 2027. The pilot added 35 a week. The first people to join are our most loyal readers, so we don't expect that pace to last. But the target needs little more than a third of it, and in 2027 we will market membership properly for the first time: [one-line marketing plan]. A newsroom of our size in [state] now gets 40% of its budget from membership, which shows this model can work at our scale. Even 700 members would match what ads are likely to earn in 2027 if their decline continues.

**Risks and how we'll manage them**
- **Year-one cash gap.** Membership will grow over the year, so we'll collect less than the full-year figure in 2027. If we grow steadily to 875, membership will bring in about $52,000 in 2027. That's roughly $15,000 less than ads would bring on their current trend. We propose covering this from [source].
- **Falling short of the target.** Our checkpoint is 500 paying members by 30 June 2027. If we're below that, I'll bring the board options at the July meeting, including bringing back sponsorships.
- **Our ad sales colleague.** [Name] has sold advertising for us part-time for 11 years. We propose [plan, e.g. offering them a role in the membership drive, where their sales experience applies directly].

**How the board can help**
Signing up members is a sales job. I'd welcome help from [board member] and [board member] in shaping the membership drive and setting targets for each quarter.
</final_document>
